(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-2-vascular-pathophysiology-quiz"]={"title":"Vascular Pathophysiology Quiz 1 — Clinical Pathophysiology I","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What are the two main pathogenic mechanisms of vasculitis?","choices":["Lipid accumulation and calcification","Immune-mediated inflammation, and pathogen invasion","Mechanical stress and loss of elastic tissue","Valve insufficiency and venous stasis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the distinction matters because the wall is being destroyed in one case by the host's own response and in the other by the organism itself. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which tissues have the highest density of capillaries?","choices":["Skin and subcutaneous fat","Myocardium and brain","Tendon and ligament","Cartilage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because capillary density tracks metabolic rate &mdash; tissues that consume the most oxygen need the shortest diffusion distances to reach it. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What do the capillaries exchange?","choices":["Oxygen alone","Blood cells into the interstitium","Fluid, nutrients, electrolytes and wastes","Nothing &mdash; capillaries only conduct blood"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the exchange runs in both directions between blood and tissues, which is why this is the only segment where the vascular system does its actual work. Hormones cross the same way. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Raised blood pressure is a risk factor for which of these?","choices":["Varicose veins and haemorrhoids","Deep vein thrombosis","Atherosclerosis, heart failure and renal failure","Arteriovenous fistula"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with cerebral haemorrhage completing the list &mdash; five different organs injured by a single haemodynamic abnormality. Cerebral haemorrhage and aortic dissection too. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"By what two routes does angiotensin II raise blood pressure?","choices":["Raising heart rate and respiratory rate","Raising smooth muscle tone, and driving aldosterone","Increasing red cell mass and blood viscosity","Dilating the efferent arteriole to raise filtration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct &mdash; one route acts on resistance immediately, the other on volume through increased renal sodium resorption, so both determinants of pressure are addressed. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What are the three components of the Virchow triad?","choices":["Immobility, dehydration and trauma","Wall damage, turbulence and hypercoagulability","Inflammation, infection and infarction","Stasis, valve insufficiency and varicosity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the triggers are multifactorial, with these three involved in varying degrees rather than all being equally present. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What is vasculitis, and which vessels does it mostly affect?","choices":["Vessel wall thickening with loss of elasticity","Wall inflammation, mostly small vessels","Dilation of a vessel from wall weakening","Thrombus formation within a deep vein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct &mdash; arterioles, capillaries and venules, although any organ and any vessel can be affected. Manifestations follow from the location of the vessel involved. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which vasculitis is classified as large vessel?","choices":["Giant cell arteritis","Polyarteritis nodosa","Kawasaki disease","Systemic lupus erythematosus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Polyarteritis nodosa and Kawasaki disease are medium vessel, and the small vessel group splits into ANCA-associated and immune complex subgroups. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What recruits smooth muscle cells into the developing lesion?","choices":["Factors released from activated platelets and macrophages","Direct contact with circulating low-density lipoprotein","Mechanical stretch from the pulse pressure alone","Complement deposition on the endothelium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and once recruited they proliferate, produce extracellular matrix, and T cells are drawn in &mdash; the lesion becomes self-sustaining. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What happens to the vessel wall in fibromuscular dysplasia?","choices":["Segments are focally thickened by hyperplasia and fibrosis","The wall thins uniformly along its whole length","Lipid-laden macrophages accumulate beneath the endothelium","Inflammatory cells destroy the wall architecture"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, in the media and intima layers, causing luminal stenosis. The exact cause is unknown, and medium and large muscular arteries are affected. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What are smooth muscle cells responsible for in response to stimuli?","choices":["Phagocytosis of lipid","Vasoconstriction or vasodilation","Production of nitric oxide to relax the vessel","Maintaining the nonthrombogenic surface"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and they also proliferate when appropriately stimulated, which is the behaviour that turns a repair response into a stenotic lesion. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which risk factor for atherosclerosis is described as the most important?","choices":["Smoking","Family history","Hyperlipidaemia","Increasing age"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct &mdash; genetics is named as the most important risk factor, ahead of the modifiable ones such as hyperlipidaemia, smoking and hypertension. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which syndrome is named for abnormal transforming growth factor signalling and defective collagen synthesis?","choices":["Kawasaki disease","Systemic lupus erythematosus","Tertiary syphilis","Marfan syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, listed under poor connective tissue quality producing weak vessel walls &mdash; a genetic route to the same wall failure that hypertension reaches mechanically. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What prevents gravitational reverse flow in veins?","choices":["Venous valves","A thick muscular media","Active pumping by the endothelium","Constriction of the adventitia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and their insufficiency is precisely what produces varicose veins, typically in the lower extremities where blood flows against gravity. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which conditions raise the risk of coagulation in deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Immobility and mechanical compression","Dehydration and thrombocytosis","Trauma and intravenous drug use","Cancer, sepsis, lupus and oral estrogen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the increased coagulability may be genetic or acquired &mdash; these four are the acquired examples given. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is the consequence of endothelial repair for nitric oxide?","choices":["Its production increases sharply","It is unchanged by injury","Its production decreases","It is replaced by carbon monoxide signalling"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and because nitric oxide is a vasodilator and an inhibitor of platelet adhesion, losing it shifts the vessel toward constriction and thrombosis. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"By what mechanism does renal artery stenosis raise blood pressure?","choices":["Direct mechanical transmission of pressure to the aorta","Autonomous aldosterone secretion independent of renin","Reduced glomerular flow and afferent pressure induce renin secretion","Loss of nitric oxide production from the renal endothelium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and blood volume and vascular tone both rise as a result &mdash; the kidney reads the stenosis as whole-body hypotension and corrects a problem that does not exist. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which two blood-borne events accompany the neointimal response?","choices":["Red cell aggregation and haemolysis","Eosinophil degranulation and mast cell activation","Plasma cell infiltration with antibody deposition","Platelet activation and leukocyte recruitment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: a thrombus forms and the inflammatory cascade begins at the injury site, so the response is simultaneously thrombotic and inflammatory. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"How is atherosclerosis characterised as a process?","choices":["A purely degenerative process of ageing","An infectious process of the arterial wall","A chronic inflammatory and healing response","A congenital malformation of the intima"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and framing it that way explains the sequence: it behaves like a repair process that never stops and progressively costs lumen. The trigger is endothelial injury. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which vessels does atherosclerosis principally affect?","choices":["Large elastic arteries such as the aorta","Small arteries and arterioles within tissues","Medium-sized muscular arteries","Capillaries"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct &mdash; the smaller branches of the aorta such as the coronary and renal arteries, roughly 3 to 4 mm. They combine elasticity with musculature to withstand high pulsatile forces and recoil to promote flow. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What characterises a saccular cerebral aneurysm?","choices":["Uniform dilation of the entire vessel circumference","A thin-walled protrusion lacking elastic lamina","A lesion arising from infected emboli","A blood-filled channel within the wall layers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with very thin or absent tunica media. The lack of elastic lamina is named as the MAIN feature, and these are typically acquired rather than congenital. The tunica media is very thin or absent. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What are the final two steps of the atherosclerotic sequence?","choices":["Lipid accumulation, then calcification","Monocyte adhesion, then platelet adhesion","Endothelial injury, then lipoprotein accumulation","T cell recruitment, then smooth muscle migration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The calcification at the end is what gives atherosclerosis its second name component &mdash; the Greek for hardening. The calcification involves matrix and necrotic debris. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What are oesophageal varices related to?","choices":["Valve insufficiency in the lower extremities","Deep vein thrombosis of the femoral vein","Cirrhosis and portal venous hypertension","Atherosclerosis of the coeliac artery"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct &mdash; dilated submucosal distal oesophageal veins. Haemorrhoids are the equivalent process at the anorectal junction. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"How does capillary diameter compare with a red blood cell?","choices":["About ten times larger","About a hundred times larger","Too variable to compare","Equal to or even slightly smaller"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the consequence is that red cells must deform to pass, which brings their membranes into close contact with the endothelium. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which are named as superficial veins?","choices":["The femoral and popliteal veins","The portal and hepatic veins","The jugular and subclavian veins","The great and small saphenous veins"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the deep veins named are the femoral, popliteal and tibial. The anatomic location determines which pathology follows. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What determines stroke volume, and therefore part of cardiac output?","choices":["Blood volume","The diameter of the arterioles","The number of capillaries in the tissue","The elasticity of the adventitia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct &mdash; sodium excretion or resorption sets the volume, which is why renal sodium handling sits at the centre of blood pressure regulation. It is regulated by sodium excretion or resorption. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which are the four principal mechanisms of vascular disease?","choices":["Inflammation, infection, infarction and ischaemia","Hypertrophy, atrophy, metaplasia and dysplasia","Thrombosis, embolism, stasis and reflux","Weakening, narrowing, dilation and rupture"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and narrowing itself can be progressive, as in atherosclerosis, or precipitous, as in thrombosis or embolism. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What happens during remodelling of a venous thrombus?","choices":["Neutrophils and macrophages infiltrate the clot","The thrombus dissolves completely with no residual change","The vein wall calcifies along its whole length","The valves regenerate and competence is restored"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, over weeks. The resulting fibrosis decreases blood flow, so the vein does not simply return to normal once the clot has organised. Collagen then replaces the fibrin. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What two quantities determine blood pressure?","choices":["Vascular resistance and cardiac output","Blood viscosity and vessel length","Heart rate and respiratory rate","Haemoglobin concentration and oxygen saturation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Resistance is regulated at the arterioles by neural and hormonal input; output is heart rate times stroke volume, with stroke volume influenced by blood volume. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What does a nonthrombogenic endothelial surface achieve?","choices":["It accelerates clot formation at rest","It prevents leukocytes from entering tissues anywhere","It stops all molecules crossing the vessel wall","It keeps blood in a fluid state"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is an active property rather than a passive one, which is why endothelial injury is the event that lets platelets adhere and a thrombus begin. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 12"}]};
